<li>Stage 5's first sentence should not contain a colon. It can read &quot;Stage 5 mostly concerns the development&#8230;&quot;; you can also omit the comma before the &quot;and&quot; in that sentence.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Capitalize &quot;French&quot; in the paragraph before the transmission log. I think changing &quot;Unknown&quot; to &quot;SCP-2381-2&quot; would help make the transmissions read clearer. If you think it's not explicit enough, you could change &quot;an unknown entity dubbed SCP-2381-2&quot; to &quot;a heretofore unknown entity (later dubbed SCP-2381-2)&quot;. You refer to it as -2 later, so I don't think changing it in the log will make a huge difference.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
My beef with this skip, and most that get to this length, is that phrases and sentences that seem to restate themselves make the article longer than it needs to be.
<p>&quot;Samples are to be cultured using YEPD medium&quot; has no significance to anyone with no experience in mycology, and even within the field is likely a distinction that wouldn't merit listing in the procedures any more than the chemicals used in the following sterilization process would be.</p>
<p>The last paragraph feels a little heavy handed and may only be necessary because the length of the article makes it easy to skim through. If you can trim down on the containment procedures where possible I think you could remove the last paragraph and have the article remain punchy.</p>
<p>No vote for now; I like the idea and I think that the depiction of the infection deserves the length and detail it was given, but I think parts of the containment could be cut shorter to help the article keep from dragging during the introduction.</p>
<p>EDIT: Changed my vote to +1, due to changes and in no small part because one of the teams was called &quot;Maz Hatters&quot;.</p>

							</div>

								
