<p>There are quite a few grammatical errors:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Should a treatment be discovered; these procedures will be reviewed and modified as needed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Comma, not semicolon.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>sodium-chloride</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not a compound word, so no hyphen.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Non-foundation personnel aware of the anomaly are to be treated with Class-B amnestics.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Capitalize Foundation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Attempts to directly communicate and dissuade SCP-2381-2 from causing future outbreaks are being researched, however; should such attempts fail, the O5 council has approved several neutralization plans.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Switch the comma and semicolon. The independent clause ends at &quot;researched,&quot; and the comma should go after &quot;however.&quot; (There are several of these sentences throughout.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The hyphae strands do this by drilling into the host cells plasma membrane, penetrating small out branches into the interior.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Host cells' (possessive) and I think you mean &quot;small out-branches,&quot; implying that the branches branch out, but it's still a little awkwardly structured.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>These structures, hereby dubbed SCP-2381-1, emit aerial spores at a constant frequency consistent with most multi-cellular fungi, however; the fruiting body never rots and continues releasing spores for an indeterminate duration.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Semicolon's in the wrong place. The clause ends after &quot;fungi&quot; so the semicolon goes there, and the comma goes after &quot;however.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>including vomiting, blood laced coughs</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Blood-laced (compound adjective).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Near the end of stage 4, infected individuals possessing a penis and testes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nothing wrong with this, just wanted to say thank you for saying &quot;people with penises&quot; instead of &quot;men&quot; because it's <em><sub>so effing easy to do in clinical writing and yet I rarely see it happen on this site</sub></em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>developing infantile spore producing structures.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Spore-producing (another compound adjective).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This process is similar in individuals possessing a functional vagina-cervix-uterus, however the clitoral glans is forced to merge with the urethral and vaginal openings, as the fruiting body develops.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Technically okay, but I think a semicolon after &quot;-uterus&quot; and comma after however would flow better clause-wise.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>undergo internal review before the ethics committee for her failure to properly contain the anomaly</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ethics Committee would be capitalized (proper noun).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Operatives complained of random interference, sudden and sometimes painful high pitched tones</p>
</blockquote>
<p>High-pitched.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Investigation of the malfunctioning equipment away from the quarantine zone concluded that the transceivers were functioning correctly, however; when returned to the mobile task force units, the previously described issues resumed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Another backwards semicolon and comma. Semicolon after &quot;correctly,&quot; comma after &quot;however.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Transmissions, consisted of a series of extended high pitch tones occurring at 30 minute intervals</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Extraneous comma, and it'd be high-pitch. (Or high-pitched.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Inquiries by lingual and radio transmission experts were able to discern a series of communications, in french</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Capitalize French.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Survey teams from Chi-7 were unable to locate the source of the transmission on the surface, however; biological analysis of the vineyard owned by &quot;patient zero&quot; discovered a number of rotting fruiting bodies next to several notably large specimens of Ash and Oak trees.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, the semicolon goes after the first clause, which ends at &quot;surface.&quot; Comma after &quot;however.&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Concept-wise, no-vote. It drags on for quite a while, but I'm not getting too much out of the concept other than surface-level body horror.</p>

